{"name":"Calypso Orchid","hex":"#d978f0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"calypso","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A style of Afro-Caribbean music that originated in Trinidad and Tobago during the early to the mid-19th century and spread to the rest of the Caribbean Antilles and Venezuela by the mid-20th century.","A song in this style of music."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To perform calypso."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/calypso"},{"term":"orchid","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant of the orchid family (Orchidaceae), bearing unusually-shaped flowers of beautiful colours. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A light bluish-red, violet-red or purple colour."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a light purple colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/orchid"}],"entries":[{"title":"Calypso bulbosa","blocks":[{"text":"Calypso is a genus of orchids containing one species, Calypso bulbosa, known as the calypso orchid, fairy slipper or Venus's slipper. It is a perennial member of the orchid family found in undisturbed northern and montane forests. It has a small pink, purple, pinkish-purple, or red flower accented with a white lip, darker purple spottings, and yellow beard. The genus Calypso takes its name from the Greek signifying concealment, as they tend to favor sheltered areas on conifer forest floors. The specific epithet, bulbosa, refers to the bulb-like corms."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Calypso_bulbosa_5493.JPG/330px-Calypso_bulbosa_5493.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calypso_bulbosa","wikidata":"Q1141063"},{"title":"Orchid","blocks":[{"text":"Orchids are plants that belong to the family Orchidaceae, a diverse and widespread group of flowering plants with blooms that are often colourful and fragrant. Orchids are cosmopolitan plants, living in diverse habitats on every continent except Antarctica. The world's richest diversity of orchid genera and species is in the tropics. Many species are epiphytes, living on trees. The flowers and their pollination mechanisms are highly specialized, attracting insect pollinators by colour, pattern, scent, pheromones, and sometimes by mimicking female insects. Orchids have very small seeds, relying on fungal partners for germination. Some orchids have no leaves, either photosynthesizing with their roots or relying entirely on fungal partners for food."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Plant_Orchid_Cymbidium_aloifolium_P1110661_05_-_cropped.jpg/330px-Plant_Orchid_Cymbidium_aloifolium_P1110661_05_-_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchid","wikidata":"Q25308"},{"title":"Orchid (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Orchid is a bright rich purple color that resembles the color which various orchids often exhibit."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Labiata.jpg/330px-Labiata.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchid_(color)","wikidata":"Q1925148"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Calypso_bulbosa_-_Flickr_005.jpg/120px-Calypso_bulbosa_-_Flickr_005.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Calypso_bulbosa_-_Flickr_005.jpg/250px-Calypso_bulbosa_-_Flickr_005.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Calypso_bulbosa_-_Flickr_005.jpg/120px-Calypso_bulbosa_-_Flickr_005.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACalypso_bulbosa_-_Flick